Introduction
Progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity and myoclonus
(PERM) is a rare variant of stiff-person syndrome (SPS)
characterized by variable expression of brainstem signs,
myoclonus, stiffness, rigidity, muscle spasms, autonomic
dysfunction, and hyperekplexia [ 1, 2]. Several eye move-
ment abnormalities, including gaze-evoked nystagmus [ 3],
prolonged saccadic latency [ 3, 4], impaired smooth pursuit
[3, 4], and supranuclear gaze palsy [ 4], have been reported
as manifestations of SPS or with autoimmunity against
glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD). However, the mani-
festations of these eye movement disorders appear closer to
PERM than to SPS, and the pathogenic role of GAD
antibodies in neurological diseases remains controversial
[5, 6].
In 2008, glycine receptor (GlyR) antibodies were ﬁrst
reported in a patient with PERM without GAD antibodies
[7], but not in the patients with SPS, leading to the
assumption that GlyR antibodies may be speciﬁc for
PERM. GlyR antibodies have subsequently been described
in further patients with PERM [ 8–10], none of whom had
GAD antibodies. GlyR antibodies bind to the native
membrane GlyR and are likely to be pathogenic. Moreover,
