it is possible that they could also be involved in the path-
ogenesis of other diseases with saccadic oscillations,
including opsoclonus or ocular ﬂutter, in which glycinergic
transmission defects have been postulated [ 11, 12].
We studied GlyR antibodies in ﬁve Japanese patients
with PERM, ocular ﬂutter syndrome (OFS), and opsoclo-
nus-myoclonus syndrome (OMS) who responded to
immunotherapy, including corticosteroids and/or immu-
nosuppressive agents. We subsequently studied a further
nine patients with adult-onset OMS for GlyR.
Patients and methods
The subjects of the main study included ﬁve Japanese adult
patients (three women, two men) with putative immune-
mediated syndromes, including PERM (case 1), OFS (cases
2 and 3), and OMS (cases 4 and 5), who were admitted to
Kitasato University Hospital between 2000 and 2010. The
diagnosis of PERM was based on (1) clinical features,
including brainstem symptoms, gaze palsy, nystagmus and
dysphagia, muscle stiffness and rigidity in the trunk and
extremities, and myoclonic jerks; (2) electrophysiological
demonstration of continuous muscle activity of agonist and
antagonist muscles; (3) abnormal CSF ﬁndings; and (4) a
high titer of GAD antibodies. The diagnosis of OMS was
based on demonstration of rapid, conjugated multidirec-
tional, horizontal, vertical, and torsional saccadic oscilla-
tions without an intersaccadic interval, while OFS was
deﬁned as a rapid, intermittent burst of conjugate hori-
zontal saccades without an intersaccadic interval [ 13].
Although OFS and OMS are likely part of the same
spectrum of saccadic oscillations, we used each term sep-
arately based on the type of eye movements that were
conﬁrmed by video recording and electronystagmography
after informed consent of the patients.
All ﬁve patients underwent brain MRI, cerebrospinal
ﬂuid (CSF) and serological examination, including thyroid
function and antibodies to ANA and SS-A/SS-B. Three of
ﬁve patients (cases 1, 2, and 5) underwent a malignancy
survey. In the patient with PERM, both serum and CSF
were serially obtained during the course of the disease for
subsequent analysis. All patients’ sera and CSF samples
obtained when the patients were admitted to our hospital
were kept frozen ( -80/C176C) until analysis.
Antibody assays
Antibodies to GlyR were measured in serum and/or CSF at
Oxford University using a previously reported cell-based
assay for screening sera at 1:20 dilution (see [ 7, 8]);
binding was scored visually on a scale from 0 to 4 with [1
considered positive (see [ 8]). Serial titrations were used to
obtain quantitative values, which are reported as titers.
GAD antibides were measured in serum or CSF at SRL,
Inc. (Tokyo, Japan) with radioimmunoassay using human
recombinant GAD65. Antibodies to classical paraneoplas-
tic antigens (Hu, Ma2, amphiphysin, CRMP5) were also
measured.
To increase the number of patients with saccadic
oscillations, we subsequently added sera from nine adult
patients with OMS (four men, ﬁve females; age
27–55 years, mean 36.6 years) that had been referred to
Oxford from hospitals in the UK.
Results
The clinical details of the ﬁve patients are given in Table 1.
Cases 1, 2, and 4 are described as representative cases of
each syndrome, and the remaining two patients are
described in the Supplementary Material.
